Answer:	6
Response:	<reasoning>
Let F be the set of students who like French fries, and B be the set of students who like burgers. We are given the following information:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The total number of students in the class is 25.
    \item The number of students who like French fries is $|F| = 15$.
    \item The number of students who like burgers is $|B| = 10$.
    \item The number of students who like both French fries and burgers is $|F \cap B| = 6$.
\end{itemize}
We want to find the number of students who do not like either food. This is the number of students who are not in $F$ and not in $B$, which can be represented as the total number of students minus the number of students who like at least one of the foods. The number of students who like at least one of the foods is given by the union of the two sets, $|F \cup B|$. We can use the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion to find $|F \cup B|$:
$$|F \cup B| = |F| + |B| - |F \cap B|$$
Substituting the given values, we have:
$$|F \cup B| = 15 + 10 - 6 = 25 - 6 = 19$$
So, 19 students like at least one of the foods (French fries or burgers or both). The number of students who do not like either food is the total number of students minus the number of students who like at least one of the foods:
$$ \text{Number of students who do not like either food} = 25 - |F \cup B| = 25 - 19 = 6 $$
Thus, there are 6 students who do not like either food.

